"ownInstaller.notInBackup" = "Not copied into the backup: on the new Mac this file must still be at the place shown above (e.g. the drive must be connected).";
"ownInstaller.include.help" = "On: the installer file itself is copied into the backup and checked like every other file, so it is available on the new Mac even without this drive and without the internet. Off: only its name, place and checksum are recorded; the file must still be there when you restore. This is about the installer only – the app's own data is chosen under Application data, and a licence only if you tick “Contains a licence or activation”.";
"ownInstaller.include.helpApp" = "An app chosen directly is always stored in the backup, packed as a zip archive that keeps its signature: outside the backup MacReplica could not check that it is unchanged.";
"ownInstaller.approve" = "Use this unverified installer";
"ownInstaller.approve.help" = "Some legitimate free apps (for example DisplayCAL) are not signed with a Developer ID, so neither macOS nor MacReplica can confirm who made them. Tick this only if you trust where you got the file, ideally the developer's own website. MacReplica records its checksum, so on the new Mac it only uses exactly this file – but that does not make it safe. Nothing in macOS is switched off: when the app first starts, macOS asks you to allow it under System Settings › Privacy & Security › Open Anyway. Leave it unticked to install the app yourself instead.";
"ownInstaller.add.help" = "Choose the installer you keep for this app: a disk image (.dmg), installer package (.pkg), zip archive (.zip) or the app itself (.app). You can add further packages, such as an activation package, in the order they are installed. Each file is checked right away; nothing in it is run. You see what it contains, whether MacReplica will use it and what happens with it on the new Mac.";
"ownInstaller.section.help" = "Downloads disappear and the new Mac may not have internet at first. If you keep installers – for example on an external drive – MacReplica records them with their checksum and can copy them into the backup. On the new Mac they are used without the internet and give you exactly the version you had. Installers are never run during the backup; disk images are only opened read-only to see which app is inside.";
