"tools.plugin.title" = "Corner plugin (Plugin Playground)";
"tools.plugin.help" = "Menus, popovers, toolbar groups and capsule buttons draw their own shapes; no setting changes them. MacReplica can load a small plugin into apps through Plugin Playground, an open-source tweak loader for Macs with Apple silicon. It needs System Integrity Protection relaxed — at least debugging allowed (csrutil enable --without debug), and on recent macOS it may need to be fully off (csrutil disable) — which only you can set in recoveryOS. “Set up automatically…” then downloads the pinned loader, installs it with MacReplica's plugin, turns on the loader's “Disable arm64e (PAC)” and starts its service. Important: that PAC setting makes the loader run every Apple-silicon (arm64e) app from an unsigned copy in /tmp — most apps work, but some crash or misbehave and have to be blacklisted. It cannot change Safari and other hardened Apple apps (they refuse injection), the system UI, or the Quick Look window on current macOS. The plugin only changes the surfaces you tick, and every row is checked against the real state of your Mac.";
"tools.plugin.needsPluginNote" = "Menus, popovers, Quick Look, toolbar groups and buttons change only once the corner plugin and its loader are set up and enabled; until then these settings do nothing.";
"tools.plugin.req.build" = "macOS build %1$@ tested with the plugin";
"tools.plugin.req.sip" = "Debugging is allowed (csrutil enable --without debug)";
"tools.plugin.req.installed" = "MacReplica's corner plugin is installed";
"tools.plugin.req.outdated" = "An older version of the corner plugin is installed – update it";
"tools.plugin.steps" = "How the setup works (at your own risk, Macs with Apple silicon only):\n1. Once, by you – allow debugging: shut down the Mac. Press and hold the power button until “Loading startup options” appears, click Options, then Continue (sign in if asked). Choose Utilities > Terminal from the menu bar, type csrutil enable --without debug and press Return. macOS confirms the change and warns that this is an unsupported configuration – that is expected. Choose Apple menu > Restart. All other protections stay on. MacReplica cannot do this step for you.\n2. Back in macOS, open this section again: “Debugging is allowed” is now green.\n3. Click “Set up automatically…” and enter your administrator password. MacReplica downloads Plugin Playground (a fixed, checksum-verified version), installs it together with MacReplica's plugin, turns on its “Disable arm64e (PAC)” setting and starts its background service. You do not need to open Plugin Playground.\n4. Every row except the last turns green. Quit and reopen apps, or restart the Mac. When you reopen MacReplica, the last row confirms that the plugin is loaded.\n5. Tick the surfaces you want above. Apps pick up a new selection the next time they open.\nTo undo: “Undo setup…” here, restart, then run csrutil enable in recoveryOS's Terminal.";
"tools.plugin.untested" = "The plugin was not tested on this macOS build. It would do nothing here, so MacReplica does not offer it until a MacReplica update has been tested on this version.";
